  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over northeast Wyoming, \nwith inverted trough stretching to the north towards northwest \nNorth Dakota. Upper level analysis places high over New Mexico \nwith ridge stretching to the north/northeast. Noted in the flow \nis a short wave pushing through eastern Montana/Wyoming into the\nDakotas. This wave, along with the aforementioned trough, are \nleading to thunderstorm development over the area.\n\nFor the rest of this afternoon into this evening, showers and\nthunderstorms will continue to develop over the area then slowly\npush towards the east. Elevated instability (CAPE values around\n2000 J/Kg) combined with very marginal shear remain in place, \nbut what continues to be notable is the steep low level lapse \nrates, and forecast downdraft CAPE values around 1500 to 1800 \nJ/Kg. Therefore, continue to believe that the primary threat \nwith any storm will by strong wind gusts, with a lower secondary\nthreat of large hail. Storms will continue into the evening, \nwith guidance continuing to show diminishing in strength and \ncoverage later in the evening. \n\nThe other ongoing issue is the hot temperatures today combined\nwith somewhat elevated humidity. Therefore, we have previously\nissued a Heat Advisory, and plan to let that continue into early\nthis evening.\n\nOn Thursday, a stronger short wave pushes into the area bringing\ngreater coverage of showers and thunderstorms, particularly to \nsouthern portions of the area per latest guidance. Shear values\nthrough the day look marginal at best, with enough instability\nto not rule out the potential for an isolated severe storm. With\nthat said, there is the potential for some locally heavy\nrainfall given the moisture in place and the possibility for\nmultiple rounds of showers/storms.\n\nTemperatures start to rebound on Friday, with the possibility \nof a stray shower/storm mainly over the north as a disturbance \nmoves through. \n\nSaturday into Sunday hot weather returns, with increasing winds\nahead of a cold front on Sunday. This elevates the fire weather\nconcerns, particularly over the west. This will bring cooler\nweather to start the work week with shower/storm chances.",
